Next year's iPhone 20 Pro models will make extensive use of 3D-printed titanium in their frames, based on the latest rumor coming out of China.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/Titanium-iPhone-15-Pro.jpg" alt="" width="2000" height="1000" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1078198" /><br/>
In a new post on Weibo, the leaker known as "Fixed Focus Digital" claims that Apple plans to manufacture parts of the device's mid-frame using a laser-based titanium powder 3D-printing process – one which would result in significantly improved durability. <br/>
<br/>
The leaker is apparently so confident in the accuracy of the rumour that they even suggested followers take screenshots of the Weibo post and reference it next year when the device is announced.<br/>
<br/>
Earlier this month, Fixed Focus Digital claimed that Apple would <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/18/iphone-20-pro-rumored-to-return-to-titanium/">return to titanium</a> for next year's 20th-anniversary iPhone models. The iPhone 18 Pro models currently use aluminum frames.<br/>
<br/>
Apple already uses the 3D-printing technique to produce the titanium cases of the Apple Watch Ultra 4 and titanium Apple Watch Series 12 models, after the company <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2025/11/mapping-the-future-with-3d-printed-titanium-apple-watch-cases/">debuted the process</a> with the Ultra 3 and Series 11. Apple also uses it for the iPhone Air's USB-C port enclosure.<br/>
<br/>
The process involves using lasers to fuse successive layers of titanium powder. Apple says this allows for more complex structural designs than conventional manufacturing methods, and significantly reduces material waste.<br/>
<br/>
The technology could give Apple greater design flexibility as it develops its rumored all-glass 20th-anniversary iPhone design, which is expected to feature <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/10/glass-centric-iphone-still-planned-2027/">curved glass and virtually invisible display bezels</a>. <em>Bloomberg</em>'s Mark Gurman has <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/04/iphone-20-biggest-revamp-since-iphone-x/">described</a> the planned changes as the biggest overhaul of the Pro line since the iPhone X.<br/>
<br/>
What remains unclear is how Apple intends to address the thermal challenges associated with titanium, given the superior heat dissipation properties of the aluminum used in its current Pro models. <br/>
<br/>
